module gunbc.auth.materialized_secret

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List }
import std.upsert_decision { ObservationVerdict, Converged, Drifted, Conflict, Inaccessible, UnknownRefused }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_ref { SecretRef }
import extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager { SmVersionState, SmEnabled, SmDisabled, SmDestroyed }
import gunbc.readback_independence {
  ReadBackProbe,
  ProbeInert,
  ProbeMayEstablish,
  readback_evidence_verdict,
  readback_probe_is_independent,
}
import gunbc.host_phase_status { observation_verdict_eq, host_phase_verdict_from }

data materialized_secret_authority_note: String = "A1's authority — the lifecycle that turns a SecretRef into a usable credential and takes it away again. What the model carried before this was the two ENDS and not the middle: extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_ref.SecretRef names a secret, gunbc.auth.secret_ref_credential.fetch_secret_ref_credential fetches its bytes, and everything between those two — bind the bytes to the transport that needs them (an ssh -i keyfile, an env var, a password on stdin), keep them 0600 and scoped, and destroy them afterwards — was performed by hand for every credential in the srv4 subsumption session. Hand-performed steps are where the fleet's credential incidents come from, and the shape of the fix is not a helper that does those steps: it is making the UNSCOPED state unwritable. So the only constructor of a MaterializedSecret is with_materialized_secret, which takes the use as a FUNCTION and returns that function's result — never the handle. A caller cannot hold a materialized secret after the bracket returns, because the bracket never yields one; the shred is the bracket's exit rather than a step someone must remember, which is the .dag reading of the plan's 'shred is by construction (the handle drop), not a remembered step'. Note what the handle deliberately does NOT carry: a path field. A transport that could read a temp path off the handle would make the raw path reachable again, and the plan's own RED is exactly 'a transport handed a raw temp path (not a MaterializedSecret handle) reds'."

data materialized_secret_binding_note: String = "The three bindings are the three the srv4 session actually performed by hand, taken from the A1 gap row verbatim rather than generalized ahead of a consumer. They are separate variants and not one 'path' because the transport contract differs in kind: SshKeyFile must be a 0600 file on disk (ssh refuses a loose-permissioned key, so the mode is part of the binding's meaning), EnvVar never touches disk and carries a name the child process reads, StdinPassword never persists at all. Collapsing them to a path would force the two non-file bindings to invent a file, which is how a secret ends up on disk that never needed to be there."

type SecretBinding
  = SshKeyFile
  | EnvVar { name: NonEmptyStr }
  | StdinPassword

type MaterializedSecret {
  ref: SecretRef
  binding: SecretBinding
}

type MaterializationRefusal
  = SecretNotLive { verdict: ObservationVerdict }
  | SecretVersionUnusable { state: SmVersionState }
  | LivenessProbeNotIndependent { probe_label: NonEmptyStr }

type MaterializationOutcome<R>
  = MaterializationCompleted { value: R }
  | MaterializationRefused { cause: MaterializationRefusal }

data secret_existence_is_not_validity_note: String = "A3, and it is A1's first slice rather than a separate probe module because liveness is a fact about a materialization, not about a secret sitting still. The incident it comes from: Secret Manager held ci-github-app-private-key at SmEnabled while the key it contained (fingerprint 0c7a15...) had been DELETED from the GitHub App, and the live key (64a8af...) existed only on the hosts. Every existence check the model could perform said yes. srv4's runner registration then 401'd, and the divergence was diagnosed and reconciled by hand. SmEnabled answers 'this version exists and is not disabled' — a fact about the STORE — and the question that matters is whether the upstream that consumes the credential still accepts it. Those are different questions and the first was standing in for the second, which is the same presupposition failure gunbc.host_swap_backing found in the swap cap: a value reported green because the condition under which it would MEAN something was assumed rather than observed."

fn secret_existence_verdict(state: SmVersionState) -> ObservationVerdict {
  match state {
    SmEnabled => Converged
    SmDisabled => Drifted
    SmDestroyed => std.upsert_decision.Absent
  }
}

data secret_liveness_qualification_note: String = "Liveness DOMINATES existence, asymmetrically, exactly as observed swap backing dominates a MemorySwapMax cap. An enabled version whose key upstream rejects is not a usable credential, and an enabled version nobody has probed is not KNOWN to be one — so the qualified verdict is Converged only when both agree, and every other liveness state passes through in place of the existence answer. The arm that matters most is the unprobed one: with no liveness observation the qualified verdict is UnknownRefused, never Converged. That is the arm that would have stopped the srv4 401 before any host was touched, because the model's existence check was green throughout the incident and would have stayed green under any amount of care."

fn secret_liveness_qualified_verdict(
  existence: ObservationVerdict,
  liveness: ObservationVerdict,
) -> ObservationVerdict {
  match liveness {
    Converged => existence
    std.upsert_decision.Absent => std.upsert_decision.Absent
    Drifted => Drifted
    Conflict => Conflict
    Inaccessible => Inaccessible
    UnknownRefused => UnknownRefused
  }
}

data github_app_jwt_mint_probe_note: String = "The liveness probe, classified under the read-back independence criterion rather than trusted. Minting a GitHub App installation token with the key is read-only against the subject: it asks GitHub whether this key is accepted and cannot MAKE a revoked key accepted, so it is ProbeInert and its Converged survives. The classification is doing real work even though it passes today, because the tempting shape for a liveness check is exactly the one that would fail it — a probe that repairs on miss (rotate the key, add a new version, re-register the app) reports success it caused, and the hand reconciliation this gap row describes was precisely that repair performed by a human. A probe that both diagnoses and repairs cannot be the thing that certifies the diagnosis."

data github_app_jwt_mint_probe: ReadBackProbe = ReadBackProbe {
  label: "mint a GitHub App installation token with the SecretRef's private key",
  subject: "the private key this SecretRef holds is the one GitHub currently accepts",
  effect_on_subject: ProbeInert,
}

data liveness_self_repairing_probe_sketch: ReadBackProbe = ReadBackProbe {
  label: "mint a token, adding a fresh key version on failure",
  subject: "the private key this SecretRef holds is the one GitHub currently accepts",
  effect_on_subject: ProbeMayEstablish {
    mechanism: "the repair arm rotates the key it is testing, so a Converged result may report the version the probe itself just wrote — the hand reconciliation of the srv4 incident, automated",
  },
}

fn secret_ref_liveness_verdict(
  probe: ReadBackProbe,
  observed: ObservationVerdict?,
) -> ObservationVerdict {
  readback_evidence_verdict(p: probe, observed: host_phase_verdict_from(observation: observed))
}

data materialization_bracket_note: String = "THE construction wall. with_materialized_secret is the only place a MaterializedSecret value is built, and it hands that value to `use` rather than returning it, so the handle's lifetime is the bracket's body by construction — there is no expression a caller can write that holds it afterwards. The refusal arm carries no handle at all, which is the same EffectsOrRefusal shape the runner-host enrollment wall uses: a refused materialization cannot be accidentally treated as a successful one, because it has nothing to treat. The three refusal causes stay separate because their remedies are different work: SecretNotLive is a credential to reconcile with its upstream, SecretVersionUnusable is a store state to fix, LivenessProbeNotIndependent is a probe to replace. HONEST RESIDUAL, the same one the enrollment wall carries: .dag has no visibility modifiers today, so a caller could hand-write MaterializedSecret { ref: .., binding: .. } and bypass this entirely. That is an explicit lie rather than an accident — it requires naming the type and building it deliberately — and it closes when node/subtree visibility grants land and this constructor becomes private to the module."

fn with_materialized_secret<R>(
  ref: SecretRef,
  binding: SecretBinding,
  existence: SmVersionState,
  probe: ReadBackProbe,
  liveness_observation: ObservationVerdict?,
  use: fn(MaterializedSecret) -> R,
) -> MaterializationOutcome<R> {
  if !readback_probe_is_independent(p: probe) {
    MaterializationRefused {
      cause: LivenessProbeNotIndependent { probe_label: probe.label },
    }
  } else {
    if !observation_verdict_eq(a: secret_existence_verdict(state: existence), b: Converged) {
      MaterializationRefused { cause: SecretVersionUnusable { state: existence } }
    } else {
      let liveness = secret_ref_liveness_verdict(probe: probe, observed: liveness_observation)
      if observation_verdict_eq(a: liveness, b: Converged) {
        MaterializationCompleted {
          value: use(MaterializedSecret { ref: ref, binding: binding }),
        }
      } else {
        MaterializationRefused { cause: SecretNotLive { verdict: liveness } }
      }
    }
  }
}

data refusal_axis_dispatch_note: String = "CORRECTION, found in review (review 42890) and it is an instance of this module's own subject matter turned on itself. The bracket originally folded existence and liveness into ONE qualified verdict and then refused with SecretNotLive whatever came out — so SecretVersionUnusable was declared and unreachable on the authority path, and two refusals with different remedies collapsed onto one shape: a destroyed store version and an upstream-revoked key both emerged as SecretNotLive { verdict: Absent }. The note two declarations above claimed the three causes stay separate BECAUSE their remedies are different work, and the code did not do it — the claim was true about the type and false about the fold. The destroyed-version witness did not catch it because it only asserted that materialization refused, never which cause it named, which is the same weak assertion this lane keeps warning about in other people's tests. The axes are now dispatched in order: the STORE axis first, because a version that is disabled or destroyed is unusable whatever any probe says about the key it used to hold, and only then the UPSTREAM axis. secret_liveness_qualified_verdict survives as the combined-STATUS authority for cell consumers that want one verdict; it is no longer what decides a materialization, because a status verdict and a refusal cause answer different questions."

data materialize_widen_red_control_note: String = "The forbidden arm as an executable declaration: materialize whenever the store says the version exists, which is what every hand-run credential step in the srv4 session effectively did. It is not a strawman — it is the behaviour that produced the 401, written down so a gate can reject it and so the authority's refusal is proven to be a decision rather than a coincidence."

fn materialize_existence_only_widen_sketch<R>(
  ref: SecretRef,
  binding: SecretBinding,
  existence: SmVersionState,
  use: fn(MaterializedSecret) -> R,
) -> MaterializationOutcome<R> {
  if observation_verdict_eq(a: secret_existence_verdict(state: existence), b: Converged) {
    MaterializationCompleted {
      value: use(MaterializedSecret { ref: ref, binding: binding }),
    }
  } else {
    MaterializationRefused { cause: SecretVersionUnusable { state: existence } }
  }
}

fn materialization_completed<R>(outcome: MaterializationOutcome<R>) -> Bool {
  match outcome {
    MaterializationCompleted { value: _ } => true
    MaterializationRefused { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

fn materialization_gate_accepts<R>(
  outcome: MaterializationOutcome<R>,
  liveness: ObservationVerdict,
) -> Bool {
  if observation_verdict_eq(a: liveness, b: Converged) {
    true
  } else {
    !materialization_completed(outcome: outcome)
  }
}

data materialized_secret_realization_frontier_note: String = "What is modeled here is the LIFECYCLE and its refusals; the host half is declared, not stubbed. The fetch leg already exists as gunbc.auth.secret_ref_credential.fetch_secret_ref_credential over the extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager AccessVersion operation, so this bracket is not re-modeling it. What does not exist is the binding leg — the 0600 scoped write, the env injection, the stdin feed, and the shred — as typed host effects, blocked on the same thing gunbc.host_swap_backing names: the argv materializer binds a hardcoded five-parameter vocabulary, so a shred is not expressible as a modeled operation today. Declaring those operations before the binding is generic would add shell-to-dag census rows no consumer can reach. The liveness OBSERVATION is likewise absent rather than faked: no GitHub App token-mint producer runs in this tree, so every liveness_observation is none today and every materialization therefore REFUSES with UnknownRefused. That is the correct state and it is deliberately loud — a lifecycle that refuses everything until a probe exists is honest, while one that materializes on existence alone is the incident."

data materialized_secret_binding_realization_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.host_effect",
    decl_name: "HostEffectTransport",
    field: WholeDeclaration,
  }
}

data materialized_secret_adc_scaffold_note: String = "A1 lands on top of a declared scaffold and says so: authenticating to Secret Manager at all currently depends on an access token the operator pastes by hand roughly hourly, because no modeled workflow identity can reach the store. That is A2 (reach-secrets ADC), already a DeclaredGap in host_standup P0:reach-secrets-network and in fleet_ssh_access. This lane does not smuggle a fix for it — the fetch leg simply inherits whatever credential is live — and A1's dependence on it is recorded here so that the day A2 lands, the thing to delete is a named row rather than an assumption nobody wrote down."
